#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092078 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "3ea5361d-2508-5d54-acbf-46a57be601f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5483" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of ![](images/39/398028421c4e26ae608c211073d010cfd7860476cec0861cb986bcc7a9cc91af.svg)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n5x_1-4x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=\\dfrac{4}{5}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/10/105c85a9f0f6bc92ac8f062d52790babc3331db1283bf4380fad442418d870eb.svg)\n\nFinally, setting $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/82/82cec52e485c08116a7e07f4b9d5b01355e8bebdd4fb910535a001e5e979a099.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092079 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "f23dcdbe-03e6-582f-8383-b07a1b2036c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5484" :question/problem "If ![](images/38/38d451c4fe8eff5b96fe2df730315c129fac48c05324c8603576a69898665e54.svg) is an eigenvector of ![](images/57/576b0cfcb7e902b8fcb9c98d25a2145f760618d9a56ed257f4828d6ab3b05064.svg)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=1$, then what is the value of $\\,\\dfrac{b}{a}\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1+5x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=-5x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/1e/1e196ae1de5d8582ec93c6f964187b9b2b85b54f3b23e4017e50a1545310348c.svg)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=-1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/67/67e700524b8c7acc8923fd97acd1bb77b391944b91c891e4ce9388b22575709b.svg)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\dfrac{-1}{5}=-\\dfrac{1}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092080 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "b59a8ad2-7233-54c2-9055-5e188e0b8503" :question/math-academy-id "e-5485" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda=1$ and $\\lambda=-4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n![](images/b1/b1b6bc95f7d29e0c64007e7c19a4690eb3def3e58d677d8a21604315bbb21c0a.svg)\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "We can get two linearly independent eigenvectors by simply finding eigenvectors corresponding to the different eigenvalues.\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/81/812c2f9dfcb3943cc13c47d2f1b85d9b1a67ce10c9e6a965a9bb915655d0316c.svg)\nSetting, for instance, $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](images/7d/7d3a9e02893b98a2739398d48138be4c930ea87b3e3577776cfdd7edf6faf4e7.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda =-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{1}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/ea/ea5ff5d281eb5376520b2763f8dd96a926d52b6485452dd60b741b48ad0d7def.svg)\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](images/db/db85af375c67eca1f1d197a284b99ef7baf9fdb543b376c27e02c3d837742b68.svg)\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n![](images/a8/a84725763b84c77f8ce4d4954fe125d5b30f735649769e1d87e700287f1c1ff6.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7b31d6-cbf3-5cfa-90f0-9eb9c7ff1c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446938} {:answer/id #uuid "3455a04a-3d31-5874-9183-71fedec9fd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446942} {:answer/id #uuid "708ac4ba-c186-57c1-8e54-0b82ac4b423d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446946} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7d641b-bdc6-59a8-8ec2-2c684ab7d2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446947} {:answer/id #uuid "c26f161b-d55c-5ad7-8a54-c13c5aa07aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "708ac4ba-c186-57c1-8e54-0b82ac4b423d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446946} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd938821-c74e-5b26-90b9-683b5bbf18c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79a5f235-c3e4-525b-8b8a-0996cfbfc3bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107107" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-1}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b9caa56-a685-5eba-85e6-58b1c539fe80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444342} {:answer/id #uuid "710b4da6-db48-5b65-bf60-9ede0d11ab35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444344} {:answer/id #uuid "93fa9520-d3fe-5440-82f2-276adae5ccf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444349} {:answer/id #uuid "d01308f3-db2f-59f6-acb9-ee0ca8f9d908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444355} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c7c06c-4453-558e-af3a-c9ca908eced4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d01308f3-db2f-59f6-acb9-ee0ca8f9d908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444355} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cd1c824-616d-5479-8fa0-71440386982d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59c071a9-0532-5356-9a7b-f9737214176f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107272" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-23 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-23 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]-(-7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-16{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-16{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=8,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c3b6eb-d05a-563e-9054-1a09875cbcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426793} {:answer/id #uuid "22e10d12-85c5-5f27-8869-ab11e85fc4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426796} {:answer/id #uuid "5abf6ee9-af9e-57ac-8483-5a3e973653e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426799} {:answer/id #uuid "69549ed7-5e3c-59e7-9843-4f93cd72fb1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426800} {:answer/id #uuid "e893480f-5916-54cc-a5ac-b814cdf8c446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e893480f-5916-54cc-a5ac-b814cdf8c446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426805} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f81bcb6-7113-5591-b120-34386b2b4833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7043291-4c2d-5218-b2ec-199ed93eadcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107275" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =8$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-(-5)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=6,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f893330-42b5-56f2-9220-00273e71b609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241750} {:answer/id #uuid "53dd0cbf-1108-5612-85c1-5408607627c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241931} {:answer/id #uuid "7007dddf-e54c-5993-b095-65efb51f09ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242178} {:answer/id #uuid "b389358f-191d-50dd-bbca-67f426593422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242746} {:answer/id #uuid "c7204dd1-3ec6-5487-b47e-cf21b00eaf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b389358f-191d-50dd-bbca-67f426593422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242746} :answer-field/id #uuid "1736c56f-53d5-57a8-ab4b-915da45ce5e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5874be66-1b30-5ade-b2d0-93cee1c2df7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107305" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =3$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e2d8f93-5ef1-55d1-b815-c1515a040b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446945} {:answer/id #uuid "a651edc0-0c43-5e03-bb3e-0a76e26d7201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446950} {:answer/id #uuid "aa601251-b637-5aff-9c87-3c9e773948b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446951} {:answer/id #uuid "b67258fc-1571-58d7-b3b9-ff66e2487d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446952} {:answer/id #uuid "f27efeb8-a34b-557e-9386-96c0b3190fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e2d8f93-5ef1-55d1-b815-c1515a040b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446945} :answer-field/id #uuid "99f559f8-f0c3-5b01-8f1d-d740f6f545ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3beff671-5436-54ac-a3ba-4902dc435008" :question/math-academy-id "q-107306" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =17$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 15 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =17,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]-17[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 \\\\ 20 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-15{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 175921860