592186243529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ec17ca8-f488-5d0d-a6c8-da1edb5aec33" :question/math-academy-id "q-107311" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =7$ and $\\lambda =29$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 18 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =29,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]-29[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-18{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2979d6f9-9e8b-5c2a-bbd2-6cb6ea061202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285151} {:answer/id #uuid "5833a572-9e0e-5859-ad31-d96beeb25e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285597} {:answer/id #uuid "a34e68f3-1c22-52d8-ace0-254e11465787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286284} {:answer/id #uuid "c76196eb-778f-5f6b-9a91-4232d319cce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286613} {:answer/id #uuid "c7dfc3f8-ec72-5328-9ecb-22f2661d9d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7dfc3f8-ec72-5328-9ecb-22f2661d9d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286618} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c50ba8d-7a5d-59de-ba5a-875076c13339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39a663bd-9526-5745-819e-7355498f25c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-18078" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=6,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{6}{-2}=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0684715f-1934-531e-a1eb-44980925a4b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446936} {:answer/id #uuid "24127ac8-a8dc-5354-a501-9b79d9e9cc82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446940} {:answer/id #uuid "51a64db1-d17d-5f2e-8521-b7c023059e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446944} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e21edc-e126-5eb1-b060-bf1813c74e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446949} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e801b2-7526-5207-9e22-764415b22da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2e21edc-e126-5eb1-b060-bf1813c74e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446949} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae3ba2a-4875-59fc-9d7d-d6fd0283faf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "301cb6c3-bb47-52a5-b9af-9305c0ba1695" :question/math-academy-id "q-18081" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector  of $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -10{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n10{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc02d67b-661f-5114-b99b-4fecf9959b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090323} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7636b6-14d7-5113-bee3-f346d2f16556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090324} {:answer/id #uuid "2e27ec4d-090a-570a-b5c5-10c16ab97587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090325} {:answer/id #uuid "3050e6c6-6028-5716-983d-b12f2b71e0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090326} {:answer/id #uuid "4651dfd7-c459-57b1-a12c-fef1de09aa88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7636b6-14d7-5113-bee3-f346d2f16556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090324} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a5fcb6f-f37e-5e57-9ee3-490fed3c5994" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1dbb2180-f084-5fd4-8f75-1faa7ff7aa82" :question/math-academy-id "q-36749" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-5}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:ans